--TEST--
A `!= null` test narrows a `?T` local to whatever dropping `null` leaves — an array, a scalar, a class — and the narrowed local is readable, indexable, writable and iterable (`rule:expressions/nullable-conversion`, ADR 0007 §§ 2 and 5)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0066: `null` is dropped on the branch the test proves, and what is
// left is the whole residue. A `?array<T>` is the shape this case exists
// for — it used to narrow nowhere at all, so every subscript on one was a
// diagnostic however carefully it had been guarded.
array<string> $seed = ["k" => "v"];
?array<string> $rows = $seed;
if ($rows != null) {
    echo $rows["k"], "\n";
    // ADR 0007 § 5: a write through the narrowed name separates and is
    // written back into the local it came out of, so the next read of it
    // sees the new element and the one before it alike.
    $rows["j"] = "w";
    echo $rows["j"], "|", $rows["k"], "\n";
    foreach ($rows as string $each) {
        echo $each;
    }
    echo "\n";
    echo Core\Arr::count($rows) as string, "\n";
}

// The narrowing ends with the guard, so the local is a `?array<string>`
// again on the other side of it — and a second test proves the same thing
// about the value the first one's write left behind.
if ($rows != null) {
    echo $rows["j"], "\n";
}

// A scalar residue narrows the same way: this is the arithmetic that had
// no representation to run on while only a class could narrow.
?int $n = 7;
if ($n != null) {
    echo ($n + 1) as string, "\n";
}
?string $s = "ab";
if ($s != null) {
    echo $s . "c", "\n";
}

// A `null` local is not narrowed by a test it fails, and the `== null`
// spelling proves the same thing about its own branch.
?array<string> $absent = null;
if ($absent == null) {
    echo "absent\n";
}
--EXPECT--
v
w|v
vw
2
w
8
abc
absent
